body:
Imagine sealing an accelerometer and a few loose objects inside a falling capsule. Relative to Earth the capsule speeds up, yet its accelerometer can read zero and the loose objects float beside it. So “accelerating towards Earth” and “feeling a force” are different observations.

The equivalence principle takes this distinction seriously: free fall, rather than rest on the ground, provides the local counterpart of inertial motion in special relativity. Local means that the laboratory is small enough, and the experiment short enough, for differences in gravity across it to be neglected.

\explanation{why-floor}{Why does standing still feel different?}{The ground pushes upward on your feet, preventing the free fall you would otherwise follow. An accelerometer measures this departure from free fall. In the falling capsule there is no comparable support force, even though its position changes rapidly in Earth-based coordinates.}

Choose an orthonormal basis in the \cref{tangent space}{math.tangent_space} at an event \(p\). This fixes the metric there to \(\eta_{\mu\nu}\), so small clock and ruler measurements have their SR form. Extending coordinates suitably into the neighbourhood also sets the first metric derivatives to zero at \(p\).

These are two distinct steps: a choice of basis fixes values, while the extension fixes first derivatives. They leave the second derivatives free to encode curvature. Thus a freely falling experiment can approximate an SR laboratory, while a larger experiment detects tides.

\explanation{point-region}{How can a point result help with a real experiment?}{Place the origin at \(p\). The Taylor expansion then begins with \(g_{\mu\nu}(x)=\eta_{\mu\nu}+O(x^2)\), because the linear terms vanish. A sufficiently small experiment suppresses those quadratic corrections; increasing its size eventually makes them measurable.}

Release two test bodies beside one another. If the gravitational field were perfectly uniform across their separation, they would acquire the same acceleration. But near a spherical mass, the nearer body accelerates inward slightly more strongly, so their separation changes even in a freely falling laboratory.

This difference is tidal gravity. It combines the idea of a \cref{freely falling observer}{gr.freely_falling_observer} with a comparison between neighbouring observers. A change of coordinates can remove the acceleration assigned to one observer at an event; it cannot make their measured relative acceleration disappear.

\explanation{tides-not-motion}{Why measure acceleration rather than separation?}{Two particles in flat spacetime can drift apart simply because they started with different velocities. A tidal experiment controls those initial velocities and asks whether the rate of separation itself changes. The extra change is what probes curvature.}

Since \(
abla g=0\), lowering an index before or after covariant differentiation gives the same result. Differentiating the inverse relation also gives \(
abla g^{-1}=0\). These identities simplify stress-energy conservation and four-velocity normalisation throughout GR.

